Now, I think it's much harder.

00:26:13.572 --> 00:26:15.507
He's been in many ways,

00:26:15.507 --> 00:26:19.177 align:start size:97% position:3%
the most conservative
president in modern America.

00:26:19.177 --> 00:26:24.182 align:start size:97% position:3%
And, but there are still a few
hardcore people who say no,

00:26:24.182 --> 00:26:27.586
it's so icky, I don't like
that we have to risk anything

00:26:27.586 --> 00:26:29.454
to try to accomplish something.

00:26:29.454 --> 00:26:31.122
But that is the
point of politics

00:26:31.122 --> 00:26:32.924
and the left is not afraid.

00:26:32.924 --> 00:26:35.894
The left is not afraid of
using the political power

00:26:35.894 --> 00:26:37.662
that is given to
them by the people.

00:26:37.662 --> 00:26:39.097
I mean that's what politics is.

00:26:39.097 --> 00:26:40.966
Politics is not abstract.

00:26:40.966 --> 00:26:43.034
Politics is not pure philosophy.

00:26:43.034 --> 00:26:44.436
It's a messy thing.

00:26:44.436 --> 00:26:46.171 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's a line from
Bismarck, he says, you know,

00:26:46.171 --> 00:26:47.672
we all like sausage but we don't

00:26:47.672 --> 00:26:49.274
want to see how the
sausage gets made.

00:26:49.274 --> 00:26:50.709
- Yeah.

00:26:50.709 --> 00:26:52.310 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And so I think conservatives
need to be more willing

00:26:52.310 --> 00:26:54.879
to take a risk.

00:26:54.879 --> 00:26:57.616
To risk how they look
in the history books,

00:26:57.616 --> 00:27:00.752 align:start size:94% position:6%
to risk maybe the
government doing something

00:27:00.752 --> 00:27:01.820
they don't like in the future,

00:27:01.820 --> 00:27:04.956
because you can't win by losing.

00:27:04.956 --> 00:27:07.459
You can't win by we're, okay
we're gonna lose this battle,

00:27:07.459 --> 00:27:08.393
we're going to lose this issue,

00:27:08.393 --> 00:27:10.161
we're gonna lose this election,

00:27:10.161 --> 00:27:12.263
but then in some distant future,

00:27:12.263 --> 00:27:13.932
when everything is
perfect and pure,

00:27:13.932 --> 00:27:15.867 align:start size:84% position:16%
then finally,
we're going to win.

00:27:15.867 --> 00:27:17.102
That's not how politics works.

00:27:17.102 --> 00:27:18.770 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So I suspect no
your answer to this,

00:27:18.770 --> 00:27:20.005
but do you think these things

00:27:20.005 --> 00:27:22.040
should be fought
on multiple fronts?

00:27:22.040 --> 00:27:25.043
Meaning there can be a
more conservative answer,

00:27:25.043 --> 00:27:26.845
there can be a more
libertarian answer

00:27:26.845 --> 00:27:28.246
that's more free market,

00:27:28.246 --> 00:27:29.981 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean that's why I started
Locals, it was like,

00:27:29.981 --> 00:27:32.350 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've had this disagreement
with Tucker Carlson

00:27:32.350 --> 00:27:35.153
on his show where he wants
the government to do it,

00:27:35.153 --> 00:27:38.189
and I said, well, I'm gonna
try the free market side.

00:27:38.189 --> 00:27:40.325 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't have a problem
with that argument

00:27:40.325 --> 00:27:42.394
because it's like maybe
we all have to hit it

00:27:42.394 --> 00:27:43.828
in different ways.

00:27:43.828 --> 00:27:44.996 align:start size:94% position:6%
Look the Prager, you
guys, that you work with,

00:27:44.996 --> 00:27:46.698 align:start size:84% position:16%
they got into a
lawsuit with YouTube.

00:27:46.698 --> 00:27:49.300
Is Dennis's or, I don't
want to speak for Dennis,

00:27:49.300 --> 00:27:51.836
but is the Prager U ethos like,

00:27:51.836 --> 00:27:54.472 align:start position:0%
let's use the law to stop
companies from doing something?

00:27:54.472 --> 00:27:56.107
Of course not, but we're all,

00:27:56.107 --> 00:27:58.376 align:start size:97% position:3%
we're all trying to
navigate very murky waters.

00:27:58.376 --> 00:28:00.145
- And isn't that the
freest market of all?

00:28:00.145 --> 00:28:02.947 align:start position:0%
Is to try all of these different
solutions to the problem.

00:28:02.947 --> 00:28:06.251
You're not going to
be able to take down

00:28:06.251 --> 00:28:09.387
this monstrous left wing edifice

00:28:09.387 --> 00:28:12.257 align:start size:84% position:16%
with one little
narrow pure solution.

00:28:12.257 --> 00:28:15.026
That one little narrow,
pure solution might help.

00:28:15.026 --> 00:28:17.896
But you're going to need a
lot of different strategies,

00:28:17.896 --> 00:28:19.931
a lot of different
tactics to undertake.

00:28:19.931 --> 00:28:22.033 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so certainly everything
should be on the table.

00:28:22.033 --> 00:28:24.703
You don't want to do anything
that is objectively wrong.

00:28:24.703 --> 00:28:26.738
I'm not advocating
that in any way.

00:28:26.738 --> 00:28:28.239
But, if--

00:28:28.239 --> 00:28:31.142
- You mean we shouldn't just
burn down the Google servers,

00:28:31.142 --> 00:28:32.310 align:start size:81% position:19%
I think they're
somewhere in Utah,

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:33.611
we shouldn't just go there and--

00:28:33.611 --> 00:28:35.480 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Light them on fire.
- Put masks on.

00:28:35.480 --> 00:28:36.681
- In the name of social justice.

00:28:36.681 --> 00:28:38.416
Yeah, no, we probably
shouldn't do that.

00:28:38.416 --> 00:28:41.586
But, but certainly, I mean
what you've done with Locals

00:28:41.586 --> 00:28:42.721
is a great example, right?

00:28:42.721 --> 00:28:45.290 align:start size:88% position:13%
Certainly we can
innovate, we can create,

00:28:45.290 --> 00:28:46.825
try to create our own
institutions, at least,

00:28:46.825 --> 00:28:48.893 align:start size:88% position:13%
create the beginnings
of our own institutions,

00:28:48.893 --> 00:28:52.263
and enforce the law that
was already on the books.

00:28:52.263 --> 00:28:55.400 align:start size:97% position:3%
Those two things are not
contradictory to one another.

00:28:55.400 --> 00:28:58.069
And actually, if we
do enforce the law,

00:28:58.069 --> 00:29:00.071
if we do have a credible
threat of enforcing law,

00:29:00.071 --> 00:29:01.606
if we do have a credible
threat of a competitor

00:29:01.606 --> 00:29:03.108
to one of these organizations,

00:29:03.108 --> 00:29:05.677 align:start size:88% position:13%
that is going to
increase the likelihood

00:29:05.677 --> 00:29:07.178
of success on all fronts.

00:29:07.178 --> 00:29:10.615
You know, when you've got
something like a Google,

00:29:10.615 --> 00:29:13.418
and when you add in all
the other tech companies,

00:29:13.418 --> 00:29:15.353
they control the
flow of internet.

00:29:15.353 --> 00:29:19.357 align:start size:88% position:13%
That is an amount of
power that I don't think

00:29:19.357 --> 00:29:22.293 align:start size:94% position:6%
anybody who is, cares
deeply about the country,

00:29:22.293 --> 00:29:23.728
or any of the founding fathers,

00:29:23.728 --> 00:29:25.363
or any people who
have thought about

00:29:25.363 --> 00:29:27.165 align:start size:84% position:16%
how this republic
is supposed to work,

00:29:27.165 --> 00:29:28.366
I don't think they
would be comfortable

00:29:28.366 --> 00:29:30.802 align:start size:88% position:13%
with that kind of power
being consolidated.

00:29:30.802 --> 00:29:33.972
Whether it's a technically
a private company or not.

00:29:33.972 --> 00:29:35.840
- Yeah, does some of your belief

00:29:35.840 --> 00:29:37.575
that these things
can be stopped,

00:29:37.575 --> 00:29:42.213 line:0%
is there a biblical sort
of bedrock beneath that?

00:29:42.213 --> 00:29:44.949 line:0%
I mean I always say, if
David could beat Goliath

00:29:44.949 --> 00:29:46.084 line:0%
then maybe David
could beat Google.

00:29:46.084 --> 00:29:47.552
Like, I really do believe that,

00:29:47.552 --> 00:29:50.321
that it is so embedded in us

00:29:50.321 --> 00:29:52.690
that the little guy
can beat the big guy,

00:29:52.690 --> 00:29:54.526
that that makes me crazy enough

00:29:54.526 --> 00:29:55.727
to try to start my
own tech company

00:29:55.727 --> 00:29:56.995 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the midst of
all this, you know?

00:29:56.995 --> 00:29:59.697
- Yeah that's such a
great point because

00:29:59.697 --> 00:30:01.466
I don't know that they can beat.

00:30:01.466 --> 00:30:04.936 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't know that
the destructive left

00:30:04.936 --> 00:30:07.305
that has wrecked
our institutions,

00:30:07.305 --> 00:30:10.708 align:start size:97% position:3%
and our political traditions,
and our civil society,

00:30:10.708 --> 00:30:13.044
I don't know that
they can be beaten,

00:30:13.044 --> 00:30:14.679
or beaten in my lifetime.

00:30:15.647 --> 00:30:17.348
But I do know I should try.

00:30:17.348 --> 00:30:19.818
And I do know I have
no reason not to try.

00:30:19.818 --> 00:30:22.954
I mean I guess that would be
maybe the biblical basis of it

00:30:22.954 --> 00:30:25.356
is what do I have to lose?

00:30:25.356 --> 00:30:29.761
Why would I, what would
I gain by playing along

00:30:29.761 --> 00:30:31.830 align:start size:84% position:16%
and saying things that
I don't believe in,

00:30:31.830 --> 00:30:33.198
being bullied into posting

00:30:33.198 --> 00:30:36.067
whatever I've got to
post on social media

00:30:36.067 --> 00:30:39.137
if I lose my integrity,
if I lose my dignity,

00:30:39.137 --> 00:30:41.172
if I lose the things
that I care about?

00:30:41.172 --> 00:30:43.241
So I have a slightly
more pleasant life,

00:30:43.241 --> 00:30:45.410 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's not a
compelling argument.

00:30:45.410 --> 00:30:46.744
- Well, I think a lot
of people watching this

00:30:46.744 --> 00:30:48.379
would say, well I don't
want to lose my job.

00:30:48.379 --> 00:30:50.715
- Yeah, and it's a real fear.

00:30:50.715 --> 00:30:52.317
- I mean, I'm sure you get
this question all the time

00:30:52.317 --> 00:30:53.885
on college campuses.

00:30:53.885 --> 00:30:56.955
Kids will say, "Hey,
I'm a conservative,

00:30:56.955 --> 00:30:58.923 align:start size:94% position:6%
"I'm a libertarian, I
believe in certain things,

00:30:58.923 --> 00:31:00.658
"but I'm afraid of
saying it in class.

00:31:00.658 --> 00:31:02.260
"Should I say it
in class or not?"

00:31:02.260 --> 00:31:05.496 align:start size:88% position:13%
I say, well, are willing
to get the bad grade?

00:31:05.496 --> 00:31:07.198
What they're asking is,

00:31:07.198 --> 00:31:11.536
how do I do what I want to
do without paying a price?

00:31:11.536 --> 00:31:12.637
You can't.

00:31:12.637 --> 00:31:15.940 align:start size:84% position:16%
You can't, everything
has a cost.

00:31:15.940 --> 00:31:18.376
For me, I've spoken out,
I've said what I believe,

00:31:18.376 --> 00:31:20.545
it has cost me certain things.

00:31:20.545 --> 00:31:22.714 align:start size:88% position:13%
I go to bed at night,
I'm grinning ear to ear,

00:31:22.714 --> 00:31:25.750 align:start size:94% position:6%
I sleep like a baby, I
think probably you do too.

00:31:25.750 --> 00:31:27.252
- Yeah.

00:31:27.252 --> 00:31:29.387
- It's worth it to me, but
there is a cost to that.

00:31:29.387 --> 00:31:31.155 align:start size:97% position:3%
- It's interesting cause I get
that question all the time,

00:31:31.155 --> 00:31:32.957 align:start size:97% position:3%
or at least when we were
allowed to travel to colleges.

00:31:32.957 --> 00:31:34.158
Remember the old days?

00:31:34.158 --> 00:31:36.861
And I once heard Ben,
Shapiro obviously,

00:31:36.861 --> 00:31:38.630
give an answer on
that which was that

00:31:38.630 --> 00:31:41.132 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you're in college that
in your core classes,

00:31:41.132 --> 00:31:42.634
so, meaning if you
want to be a doctor,

00:31:42.634 --> 00:31:45.103
in your core classes you
probably shouldn't speak up

00:31:45.103 --> 00:31:47.038 align:start size:84% position:16%
because you do
need to move ahead,

00:31:47.038 --> 00:31:48.539 align:start size:81% position:19%
and you don't want
to be screwed,

00:31:48.539 --> 00:31:49.841 align:start size:94% position:6%
but in some of your other
stuff, you know like,

00:31:49.841 --> 00:31:52.277
lesbian dance theory,
like there you maybe

00:31:52.277 --> 00:31:53.578
could speak up a little bit more

00:31:53.578 --> 00:31:54.545
because it probably
won't take you out.

00:31:54.545 --> 00:31:56.080
There was some version of that,

00:31:56.080 --> 00:31:57.382
I'm not totally sure if
that's what he feels now,

00:31:57.382 --> 00:31:59.751
and for like four
or five years ago,

00:31:59.751 --> 00:32:01.986 align:start size:97% position:3%
what I would say is, just
suck it up and get the grade

00:32:01.986 --> 00:32:03.488
because you want to get out.

00:32:03.488 --> 00:32:05.323 align:start size:88% position:13%
But about four years
ago I completely changed

00:32:05.323 --> 00:32:06.791
and I started saying
the absolute reverse,

00:32:06.791 --> 00:32:08.159
you must speak up.
- [Michael] Yeah.

00:32:08.159 --> 00:32:10.061
- If you don't speak up,

00:32:10.061 --> 00:32:12.263
if you're in college, the world

00:32:12.263 --> 00:32:14.299
will never be easier for you.

00:32:14.299 --> 00:32:15.833
You're not going to
get out of college,

00:32:15.833 --> 00:32:16.968
you're not gonna
get out of college

00:32:16.968 --> 00:32:18.503 align:start size:88% position:13%
and have a car payment,
and a mortgage,

00:32:18.503 --> 00:32:21.105
and a wife, and a dog, and
all these responsibilities

00:32:21.105 --> 00:32:23.174
plus all the endless
nonsense of the world,

00:32:23.174 --> 00:32:26.311 align:start position:0%
and suddenly be like, you know,
I'm gonna get brave today.

00:32:26.311 --> 00:32:28.046
- Time to lose my job.

00:32:28.046 --> 00:32:29.514
- Let's roll, yeah.

00:32:29.514 --> 00:32:30.982
So you'll never do it.

00:32:30.982 --> 00:32:33.584 align:start size:97% position:3%
So that's what I push now,
but it doesn't mean it's easy.

00:32:33.584 --> 00:32:35.153
In fact, actually, it
means it's the reverse.

00:32:35.153 --> 00:32:37.388 align:start size:88% position:13%
- This is the same
argument as don't worry

00:32:37.388 --> 00:32:39.524
about the snowflakes when
they get to the real world.

00:32:39.524 --> 00:32:42.994 align:start size:97% position:3%
No, the real world gets
realer and realer and realer.

00:32:42.994 --> 00:32:46.197 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I understand
prudence is a virtue,

00:32:46.197 --> 00:32:48.032
and politics changes with time.

00:32:48.032 --> 00:32:49.901
Politics is about circumstances.

00:32:49.901 --> 00:32:52.937 align:start size:84% position:16%
So maybe it made more
sense at one time

00:32:52.937 --> 00:32:54.605
to keep a little bit quiet,

00:32:54.605 --> 00:32:55.873
and then you can
try to move here,

00:32:55.873 --> 00:32:58.376
but in terms of how
I would live my life,

00:32:58.376 --> 00:32:59.677
or the advice I would give,

00:32:59.677 --> 00:33:01.179
no, I would say, speak up.

00:33:01.179 --> 00:33:02.814 align:start size:88% position:13%
Doesn't mean you need
to go wear your MAGA hat

00:33:02.814 --> 00:33:04.315
every time or at
the watercooler,

00:33:04.315 --> 00:33:06.417
but it does mean if you
were asked a question

00:33:06.417 --> 00:33:09.287
about your beliefs, I would
tell people what you think.

00:33:09.287 --> 00:33:12.557
I think it's a good
habit to get into.

00:33:12.557 --> 00:33:14.192
Let's not forget that
virtues are habits.

00:33:14.192 --> 00:33:17.395 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so if you, if you practice
them from a young age,

00:33:17.395 --> 00:33:18.963 align:start size:88% position:13%
you're more likely to
be able to practice them

00:33:18.963 --> 00:33:19.931
in older age.
- [Dave] Right.

00:33:19.931 --> 00:33:21.165
- And if you don't, if you say,

00:33:21.165 --> 00:33:23.334
"No when I turned 60,
then I'm going to start

00:33:23.334 --> 00:33:24.869
"practicing the virtues."

00:33:24.869 --> 00:33:26.304
I don't know if it's gonna work.

00:33:26.304 --> 00:33:27.905
- And the scarier
part is that it's,

00:33:27.905 --> 00:33:29.207
I think a lot of
young people think

00:33:29.207 --> 00:33:31.609
oh it's when I turn 22, right?

00:33:31.609 --> 00:33:34.045 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, 60, we can all
accept that a 60 year old,

00:33:34.045 --> 00:33:36.180 align:start size:84% position:16%
most likely, you
know, although maybe

00:33:36.180 --> 00:33:38.116
he's going through like
a later midlife crisis,

00:33:38.116 --> 00:33:40.051
and then maybe is going
to do something nuts,

00:33:40.051 --> 00:33:42.720 align:start size:97% position:3%
but that probably involves
going to Vegas and, you know--

00:33:42.720 --> 00:33:44.355 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, not practicing
the virtues.

00:33:44.355 --> 00:33:46.958
- Right, you're not gonna
go with the virtue thing.

00:33:46.958 --> 00:33:51.095
You sort of hit on this, but
I'm curious what you think

00:33:51.095 --> 00:33:52.964
the sort of future, how wide

00:33:52.964 --> 00:33:55.767 align:start size:84% position:16%
a future conservative
tent can be.

00:33:55.767 --> 00:33:58.136
Because I'll drop a little
knowledge for you Knowles,

00:33:58.136 --> 00:33:59.737
I'm working on my second book,

00:33:59.737 --> 00:34:02.573 align:start position:0%
and that's kind of the direction
I'm going to go with it.

00:34:02.573 --> 00:34:05.576
That what I'm really, it
seems to me that the right,

00:34:05.576 --> 00:34:07.545
broadly speaking, has
won the big arguments.

00:34:07.545 --> 00:34:09.380
They've won the arguments
on individual liberty,

00:34:09.380 --> 00:34:11.182 align:start size:84% position:16%
they've won the
arguments on family,

00:34:11.182 --> 00:34:15.987
on belief, at least, in terms
of as an organizer of society,

00:34:15.987 --> 00:34:18.089 align:start size:88% position:13%
maybe not at a absolute
individual level,

00:34:18.089 --> 00:34:19.357 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think we have a little
disagreement on that,

00:34:19.357 --> 00:34:21.426
which I'm happy to talk about,

00:34:21.426 --> 00:34:23.528
but that basically got
the big stuff right.

00:34:23.528 --> 00:34:24.896
- [Michael] Yeah.

00:34:24.896 --> 00:34:26.097
And what I think is for someone

00:34:26.097 --> 00:34:28.166
that's on the more
liberal side of that,

00:34:28.166 --> 00:34:30.168
my job is to kind of
make sure that they

00:34:30.168 --> 00:34:33.771
never go off the deep end
too far right, let's say,

00:34:33.771 --> 00:34:35.907 align:start size:88% position:13%
and start coming around
to like, you know,

00:34:35.907 --> 00:34:37.742
trying to get violence
out of video games again

00:34:37.742 --> 00:34:39.143 align:start size:81% position:19%
like the right
used to try to do.

00:34:39.143 --> 00:34:40.578
- [Michael] Right.

00:34:40.578 --> 00:34:42.180 align:start size:94% position:6%
- But I know that there's
people on the right,

00:34:42.180 --> 00:34:44.982 align:start size:84% position:16%
that you know, want
more religious version
of conservatism,

00:34:44.982 --> 00:34:46.417 align:start size:84% position:16%
then there's obviously
the libertarians.

00:34:46.417 --> 00:34:49.287 align:start size:94% position:6%
What do you, do you think
that thing can hold?

00:34:49.287 --> 00:34:51.122 align:start size:88% position:13%
Because I really
believe that the future

00:34:51.122 --> 00:34:52.957
of Western politics,
not just in America,

00:34:52.957 --> 00:34:56.294
in every Western nation
is a center right future.

00:34:56.294 --> 00:34:58.629 align:start position:0%
I think we're seeing that happen
across the world right now.

00:34:58.629 --> 00:34:59.864
- Yes.

00:34:59.864 --> 00:35:01.032
- But how wide do you
think that thing can be

00:35:01.032 --> 00:35:03.201
to not to become too big?

00:35:03.201 --> 00:35:05.470
- I think you don't
need to worry as much

00:35:05.470 --> 00:35:08.873
about the breadth as the object,

00:35:08.873 --> 00:35:10.608
what you're fighting against.

00:35:10.608 --> 00:35:12.043
Because I do think that there

00:35:12.043 --> 00:35:13.911
is a lot of space on the right.

00:35:13.911 --> 00:35:15.980
But it's not going
to be the same tent

00:35:15.980 --> 00:35:18.316 align:start size:88% position:13%
that we saw in the
Cold War, for instance.

00:35:18.316 --> 00:35:20.384 align:start size:84% position:16%
So you will never
find a greater admirer

00:35:20.384 --> 00:35:23.488
of William F. Buckley Jr.,
and Ronald Reagan, than me.

00:35:23.488 --> 00:35:24.422
Okay?

00:35:24.422 --> 00:35:25.590
I really admire those guys.

00:35:25.590 --> 00:35:26.858
They fought their battles.

00:35:26.858 --> 00:35:28.192
They were fighting
the Soviet Union,

00:35:28.192 --> 00:35:30.294 align:start size:88% position:13%
and they cobbled
together this coalition

00:35:30.294 --> 00:35:31.829
that doesn't make
a lot of sense.

00:35:31.829 --> 00:35:33.631
The coalition was the
social conservative

00:35:33.631 --> 00:35:35.233
traditionalist religious right,

00:35:35.233 --> 00:35:38.402 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they hated of the
atheism of the Soviet Union.

00:35:38.402 --> 00:35:41.706 align:start size:88% position:13%
You've got the
libertarians, you know,

00:35:41.706 --> 00:35:45.109 align:start size:97% position:3%
practical anarcho capitalists
on one side, right?

00:35:45.109 --> 00:35:46.777
They don't really care
about the religious right

00:35:46.777 --> 00:35:48.012
argument at all, but they hate

00:35:48.012 --> 00:35:49.614
the collectivism of
the Soviet Union.

00:35:49.614 --> 00:35:51.749 align:start size:84% position:16%
You've got the
blue dog Democrats,

00:35:51.749 --> 00:35:53.718
they don't really care about
either of those two things,

00:35:53.718 --> 00:35:56.387
they care about the Imperial
expansion of the Soviet Union.

00:35:56.387 --> 00:35:58.089 align:start size:88% position:13%
They come together,
they fight the Cold War.

00:35:58.089 --> 00:35:59.323
- Where do you think
Reagan fits in that?

00:35:59.323 --> 00:36:01.092 align:start size:88% position:13%
He's more of like a
California conservative

00:36:01.092 --> 00:36:03.427 align:start size:97% position:3%
that kind of shifted, right,
in the midst of all that,

00:36:03.427 --> 00:36:04.362
right?

00:36:04.362 --> 00:36:06.297
- Anybody who tells you they can

00:36:06.297 --> 00:36:09.100 align:start size:84% position:16%
analyze Ronald Reagan
is lying to you.

00:36:09.100 --> 00:36:11.002
Ronald Reagan is one
of the most enigmatic

00:36:11.002 --> 00:36:12.737
figures in American politics.

00:36:12.737 --> 00:36:13.905
I mean this is a guy,

00:36:13.905 --> 00:36:16.073 align:start size:88% position:13%
he liberalized abortion
laws in California

00:36:16.073 --> 00:36:17.708
more than anybody before him,

00:36:17.708 --> 00:36:19.610 align:start size:81% position:19%
and yet he becomes
one of the most

00:36:19.610 --> 00:36:21.779
pro-life presidents
in American history.

00:36:21.779 --> 00:36:25.149 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's very, I mean I
know that Bill Buckley

00:36:25.149 --> 00:36:28.953 align:start position:0%
had a major influence on Ronald
Reagan over to the right.

00:36:28.953 --> 00:36:30.454
I mean, I think there
were a lot of people

00:36:30.454 --> 00:36:33.191
of his generation who said
they got mugged by reality.

00:36:33.191 --> 00:36:35.826
And I think that's how you
go from the New Deal democrat

00:36:35.826 --> 00:36:37.828
into a Reaganite conservative.

00:36:37.828 --> 00:36:40.464 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I mean it's what's happening
to these people right now.

00:36:40.464 --> 00:36:41.666
- Right, it's happening now.

00:36:41.666 --> 00:36:44.035 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so I think that made
a lot of sense then.

00:36:44.035 --> 00:36:46.771
I don't think that makes
as much sense right now.

00:36:46.771 --> 00:36:49.874 align:start size:97% position:3%
A lot of that kind of
conservatism took on a very,

00:36:49.874 --> 00:36:52.143
especially in the 80s
and 90s and 2000s,

00:36:52.143 --> 00:36:55.546
an economics first
dimension to it.

00:36:55.546 --> 00:36:58.216
People just, they said forget
about any cultural issues,

00:36:58.216 --> 00:37:01.886
any social issues, we're just
going to talk about tax rates.

00:37:01.886 --> 00:37:04.422 align:start size:97% position:3%
Yeah, that's what gets people
riled up in the mornings.

00:37:04.422 --> 00:37:07.058
Slightly lowering the
marginal tax rate.

00:37:07.058 --> 00:37:11.329 align:start position:0% line:0%
And it's ironic that
conservatism devolved into that

00:37:11.329 --> 00:37:14.632 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
because the great
philosopher of conservatism,

00:37:14.632 --> 00:37:15.866 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
probably the founder
of what you call

00:37:15.866 --> 00:37:17.902 line:0%
modern conservatism
is Edmund Burke,

00:37:17.902 --> 00:37:20.238 line:0%
and in the most
often quoted part

00:37:20.238 --> 00:37:22.406 line:0%
of the most often
quoted book of his,

00:37:22.406 --> 00:37:24.242 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Reflections On The
Revolution in France,"

00:37:24.242 --> 00:37:27.612 align:start size:84% position:16%
he said, "The age
of chivalry is gone.

00:37:27.612 --> 00:37:30.881 align:start size:97% position:3%
"that of sophisters,
economists, and calculators

00:37:30.881 --> 00:37:32.817
"has succeeded, and
the glory of Europe

00:37:32.817 --> 00:37:33.884
"is extinguished forever."

00:37:33.884 --> 00:37:35.386
And what have we do?

00:37:35.386 --> 00:37:37.054 align:start size:97% position:3%
We become sophisters,
economists, and calculators.

00:37:37.054 --> 00:37:38.556
- [Dave] We double down.

00:37:38.556 --> 00:37:40.391 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, I don't think that
the future of the right

00:37:40.391 --> 00:37:43.294
is a bunch of egghead
people on spreadsheets

00:37:43.294 --> 00:37:46.764
talking about how to more
efficiently move markets.

00:37:46.764 --> 00:37:48.699
- But you think those
people are part of it?

00:37:48.699 --> 00:37:50.268
It's just not the heart of
it, something like that?

00:37:50.268 --> 00:37:51.936
- Yes, yeah, I mean, I think

00:37:51.936 --> 00:37:53.337 align:start size:81% position:19%
they certainly
can be part of it.

00:37:53.337 --> 00:37:54.572
- Yeah.

00:37:54.572 --> 00:37:57.508
- But I think what we've
got to be motivated by

00:37:57.508 --> 00:37:59.644
is the threat, and
the threat right now

00:37:59.644 --> 00:38:01.045
is not the Soviet Union.

00:38:01.045 --> 00:38:04.248
The threat is the woke
left, the radical left,

00:38:04.248 --> 00:38:08.185
that is literally burning
down cities, as we speak.

00:38:08.185 --> 00:38:10.354
Now, who opposes the woke left?

00:38:10.354 --> 00:38:14.358
The religious right,
Catholic integralist,

00:38:14.358 --> 00:38:17.561 align:start position:0%
you know, as far social
conservative as you can imagine,

00:38:17.561 --> 00:38:19.397
and Dave Rubin.

00:38:19.397 --> 00:38:21.198
Last time I checked, you're
not a Catholic integralist.

00:38:21.198 --> 00:38:22.600
Not yet at least.

00:38:22.600 --> 00:38:27.371 align:start size:97% position:3%
And yet, both of those
groups fit perfectly together

00:38:28.539 --> 00:38:30.975
in this tent because we
do have a common foe.

00:38:30.975 --> 00:38:35.546 align:start size:94% position:6%
The politics of opposition
is often derided,

00:38:35.546 --> 00:38:37.548 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the politics of
opposition works pretty well.

00:38:37.548 --> 00:38:38.683
It worked well in World War II,

00:38:38.683 --> 00:38:39.850
it worked well in the Cold War.

00:38:39.850 --> 00:38:41.752 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Do you think there's
any risk of that

00:38:41.752 --> 00:38:43.087
in the long term
because then you

00:38:43.087 --> 00:38:44.889 align:start size:84% position:16%
sort of get these
competing interests?

00:38:44.889 --> 00:38:47.291
I sense this, I sense
this more from the

00:38:47.291 --> 00:38:48.326
Catholic side of this.

00:38:48.326 --> 00:38:49.560
- [Michael] Right.

00:38:49.560 --> 00:38:51.095
That they're gonna
align themselves

00:38:51.095 --> 00:38:52.029
with some people that they
really don't want to like

00:38:52.029 --> 00:38:53.898
for this sort of short term win,

00:38:53.898 --> 00:38:55.666
even if we all agree that yes,

00:38:55.666 --> 00:38:57.368
that's the thing we gotta fight,

00:38:57.368 --> 00:38:58.502
but that in the long term,

00:38:58.502 --> 00:39:00.404 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's not a coalition
that can hold.

00:39:00.404 --> 00:39:02.640
- Well in the long term,
we're all dead, I guess,

00:39:02.640 --> 00:39:04.375
as a famous economist once said.

00:39:04.375 --> 00:39:05.476
I mean.
- Yeah.

00:39:05.476 --> 00:39:08.045
- I think this anxiety comes

00:39:08.045 --> 00:39:10.014
from some of the
same pathologies

00:39:10.014 --> 00:39:12.550 align:start size:94% position:6%
that have afflicted
politics in recent years,

00:39:12.550 --> 00:39:15.219
which is that we think
politics is eternal.

00:39:15.219 --> 00:39:16.721
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:39:16.721 --> 00:39:19.190
- And for the left, politics
has become a sort of religion,

00:39:19.190 --> 00:39:20.858
politics is not eternal.

00:39:20.858 --> 00:39:23.494
Politics is just how
we all get together

00:39:23.494 --> 00:39:25.162
in this civil society of ours,

00:39:25.162 --> 00:39:26.697
and politics changes a lot.

00:39:26.697 --> 00:39:29.033 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's changed a lot in
just the last four years.

00:39:29.033 --> 00:39:30.835
So right now we
have this threat,

00:39:30.835 --> 00:39:32.903 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we've got this coalition
that's come together.

00:39:32.903 --> 00:39:34.305
Good.

00:39:34.305 --> 00:39:35.906
Let's do it, man.

00:39:35.906 --> 00:39:38.042 align:start size:81% position:19%
I'm not gonna lose
sleep at night

00:39:38.042 --> 00:39:39.577 align:start size:84% position:16%
because of some
hypothetical problem.

00:39:39.577 --> 00:39:43.381
If in the future, a different
political circumstance arises,

00:39:43.381 --> 00:39:45.282 align:start size:81% position:19%
then I suspect,
